---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import AccordionItem from '../components/AccordionItem.astro';
---

<Layout title="FAQ -- mailypoppins" description="Frequently asked questions and troubleshooting for mailypoppins.">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content" class="content page">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Common questions and troubleshooting.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 id="general">General</h2>
      <div class="accordion-group">
        <AccordionItem question="Config file not found">
          <p>
            Run <code>mp config init</code> to create the config file at
            <code>~/.config/mailypoppins/config.toml</code>. The interactive wizard will walk you through
            SMTP/IMAP credentials, test connections, discover server mailboxes, and assign roles.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="Password not found in keyring">
          <p>
            Run <code>mp config set-password</code> to store your SMTP or IMAP password in the
            OS keyring (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, or Linux Secret Service).
            Passwords are never stored in the config file.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="Email not approved for sending">
          <p>
            The draft workflow requires explicit approval before sending. Run
            <code>mp mark-approved &lt;selector&gt;</code> or press <code>A</code> in the TUI to
            change a draft's status from <code>draft</code> to <code>approved</code>. Only approved
            emails can be sent. To reverse an accidental approval, press <code>D</code> in the TUI
            (or run <code>mp mark-draft &lt;selector&gt;</code>) and the draft is editable again.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="SMTP authentication failed">
          <p>
            Check your credentials with <code>mp config show</code> and re-run
            <code>mp config set-password smtp</code> if needed. For OAuth2 accounts, try
            <code>mp config oauth2-login</code> to refresh the token.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="Signature file not found">
          <p>
            Check the path in your config under <code>[signatures]</code>. Paths support
            <code>~</code> expansion. Verify the file exists at the specified location with
            <code>ls</code> or <code>mp config show</code>.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="oauth2-exchange">OAuth2 / Exchange</h2>
      <div class="accordion-group">
        <AccordionItem question="IMAP is disabled for my organization">
          <p>
            Your Microsoft 365 tenant may have IMAP disabled. Contact IT to enable it, or use
            <code>auth_method = "graph"</code> in your account config, which bypasses IMAP/SMTP
            entirely and uses the Microsoft Graph API for all mail operations.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="Needs admin consent for API permissions">
          <p>
            The <code>IMAP.AccessAsUser.All</code> and <code>SMTP.Send</code> permissions often
            require admin consent. Request your IT administrator to grant consent via Azure portal
            &gt; Entra ID &gt; App registrations &gt; API permissions.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="OAuth2 token expired">
          <p>
            Run <code>mp config oauth2-login --account &lt;name&gt;</code> to re-authenticate.
            Access tokens expire after roughly one hour and are auto-refreshed, but refresh tokens
            expire after approximately 90 days of inactivity.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="AUTHENTICATE failed with OAuth2">
          <p>
            This can mean IMAP is disabled for the account, the token has wrong scopes, or admin
            consent is missing. Check the app permissions in Azure and ensure IMAP is enabled. If
            your tenant blocks IMAP, consider switching to <code>auth_method = "graph"</code>.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="sync-workflow">Sync and Workflow</h2>
      <div class="accordion-group">
        <AccordionItem question="How does sync work?">
          <p>
            <code>mp sync</code> is additive by default -- it only downloads new emails,
            deduplicating by Message-ID. It uses a two-pass approach: first fetches headers (~50
            bytes per message) to check what is new, then downloads full messages only for emails
            not already stored locally.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="What happens to messages deleted on the server?">
          <p>
            The same sync pass prunes them. Every sync enumerates the whole mailbox even when
            it downloads only the newest messages, so a message the server no longer lists is
            removed from the local store whatever its age, and a mailbox cleaned up in another
            client converges on the next sync.
          </p>
          <p>
            One pass holds removals back: the one that could not bring in everything that landed
            since the previous sync, because a message missing from the store is not evidence that
            it is gone from the server. The status line says so when it happens
            (<code>N removal(s) held back</code>), and the next complete pass applies them. A
            first sync is such a pass by definition, since nothing local is known yet: that covers
            a brand-new account, and equally the first sync of an account you have had for months
            after an upgrade rebuilds its local store.
          </p>
          <p>
            A message that was <em>moved</em> converges as soon as the sync has read the copy in
            its destination. On servers that issue a new identifier on a move (Exchange, Dovecot)
            that is the same pass. On Gmail, archiving only removes the Inbox label and the copy
            in All Mail keeps its original, old identifier, so a capped sync will not see it
            again: the inbox row goes and the archived copy is re-filed by the next full sync
            (<code>S</code> in the TUI).
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="Can I use mailypoppins with multiple accounts?">
          <p>
            Yes. Use the <code>[[accounts]]</code> array in <code>config.toml</code>. Each account
            has its own SMTP/IMAP settings, local store, and mailboxes. Switch between accounts in
            the TUI with the backtick key (<code>`</code>) or <code>Ctrl+1-9</code>.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="How are attachments handled?">
          <p>
            Incoming attachments are stored as blobs beside the message row, not as files you
            browse. Use <code>o</code> in the TUI to open one (it is written to a private temp
            directory first), <code>O</code> to save it to a directory you choose, or
            <code>mp save</code> from the CLI. For outgoing drafts, list file paths in the
            <code>attachments:</code> frontmatter field, or point one entry at a folder to attach
            every file inside it; forwarding a message
            materialises its attachments under the account's
            <code>attachments/</code> directory and lists those paths for you.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>

        <AccordionItem question="Does it support HTML emails?">
          <p>
            Outgoing emails are written in Markdown and converted to HTML for sending, with a
            plaintext fallback. For incoming mail both parts are kept: the text the TUI shows and
            the original HTML, each as a blob beside the message row. Press <code>b</code> in the
            TUI to open the HTML version in your browser.
          </p>
        </AccordionItem>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: var(--space-16);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }

  .page-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-subtext0);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .accordion-group {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
